<Sequential id="interactive_comm" title="Interactive Communication">

  <!-- ============================================================
       PART 1: WHY INTERACTIVE?
       ============================================================ -->



<Markdown  id="olx_tutorial_welcome" title="Welcome"><![CDATA[
# Welcome!

Your course staff and I are running an experiment. This tutorial will help you understand what it is and why we're doing it — and by the end, you'll be creating interactive content yourself.

Your first job:
* Find the **Next** button and hit it.
* Follow the instructions on the next page.
]]></Markdown>


<Vertical id="icap_intro_section" title="Active Learning">
<MarkupProblem id="icap_intro" title="How People Learn">
Great! Welcome to the next page. Quick question:

When well-structured, what's most effective for learning?

( ) **Passive**: Reading a textbook or watching a lecture
( ) **Active**: Answering a question (like this one)
( ) **Constructive**: Writing an essay or explanation
(x) **Interactive**: Discussing, debating, or teaching someone

[explanation]
Researchers Michelene Chi and Ruth Wylie proposed the **ICAP framework** (2014), which ranks learning activities:

**I**nteractive ≥ **C**onstructive > **A**ctive > **P**assive

Each level involves deeper cognitive engagement. As defined by Chi:

* Passive means receiving -- reading text, watching a video, or listening to a lecture.
* Active means doing something with the material.
* Constructive means producing something new.
* Interactive means doing so *with others* — building on each other's ideas.

The key prediction: **I ≥ C > A > P** for learning outcomes. The sharpest boundary is between Passive and Active — the mere act of engaging with material rather than passively receiving it produces the largest gains. This has been validated across countless studies.                                                                                                  

Richard Hake studied 6,000 physics students across 62 courses (1998) and found that classes using **interactive engagement** produced learning gains roughly **twice** those of traditional lectures. A massive meta-analysis of 225 studies (Freeman et al., 2014, published in *PNAS*) confirmed: students in traditional lectures are **1.5 times more likely to fail** than students in active learning environments.

The pattern holds across disciplines, age groups, and cultures. Interactive beats passive.
[/explanation]
</MarkupProblem>
<Collapsible id="icap_detail" title="Citations">
<Markdown><![CDATA[
### Hake (1998)

- Hake, R. R. (1998). *Interactive-engagement versus traditional methods: A six-thousand-student survey of mechanics test data for introductory physics courses*. American Journal of Physics, 66(1), 64–74. https://stemteachersnyc.org/wp-content/uploads/2015/03/HAKE-INTERACTIVE-ENGAGEMENT-VS-TRADITIONAL-1.pdf

**Summary:**
- Large-scale study (6,000+ students) comparing traditional lectures vs. interactive engagement (IE).
- IE methods produced significantly higher conceptual learning gains (~0.48 vs. ~0.23).
- Demonstrates strong benefits of active, student-centered instruction.

---

### Freeman et al. (2014)

- Freeman, S., Eddy, S. L., McDonough, M., Smith, M. K., Okoroafor, N., Jordt, H., &amp; Wenderoth, M. P. (2014). *Active learning increases student performance in science, engineering, and mathematics*. Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences, 111(23), 8410–8415. https://www.pnas.org/doi/10.1073/pnas.1319030111

**Summary:**
- Meta-analysis of 225 STEM studies comparing active learning vs. lecturing.
- Active learning improved exam scores (~6%) and reduced failure rates.
- Students in traditional lectures were about 1.5× more likely to fail.
- Concludes active learning is consistently more effective across contexts.

---

### Chi &amp; Wylie (2014)

- Chi, M. T. H., &amp; Wylie, R. (2014). *The ICAP framework: Linking cognitive engagement to active learning outcomes*. Educational Psychologist, 49(4), 219–243. https://files.eric.ed.gov/fulltext/EJ1044018.pdf

**Summary:**
- Introduces the ICAP framework (Interactive, Constructive, Active, Passive).
- Learning effectiveness increases with deeper cognitive engagement.
- Interactive and constructive activities lead to better outcomes than passive ones.
- Provides a theoretical explanation for why active learning works.
]]></Markdown>
</Collapsible>
</Vertical>




<MarkupProblem id="writing_comm" title="How Does This Class Teach Communication?">
So: interactive learning works. But how do most writing classes teach you to communicate?

(x) For passive readers :(
( ) Interactively, for the 21st century!

[explanation]
We'll break that, right here, right now.

Notice what we've been doing? We've been replacing a *lecture* or *reading* with a series of *leading questions*. Each question has an obvious answer, but answering it forces you to *think* rather than just *read*.

That's not rocket science. It's one of the oldest teaching techniques (Socrates was doing it 2,400 years ago). But digital technology lets us do it at scale — and it lets *you* do it too.

By the end of this tutorial, you'll be authoring interactive content like this yourself.

Footnote: While writing generates output which is (usually) consumed **passively**, the act of writing is **constructive**. Writing-to-learn can work **incredibly** well.
[/explanation]
</MarkupProblem>



<!-- Inspiration gallery — moved before authoring per plan -->
<Vertical id="inspiration" title="Inspiration">
<Markdown><![CDATA[
## Interactive Communication in the Wild

Before you start authoring, let's look at what's possible. These examples all communicate ideas *interactively* — not through passive text, but through experiences:

* [**Parable of the Polygons**](https://ncase.me/polygons/) — How small individual biases create large-scale segregation. You move the shapes yourself and watch the pattern emerge. (Nicky Case &amp; Vi Hart)
* [**How to Remember Everything Forever-ish**](https://ncase.me/remember/) — Spaced repetition explained through a playable comic. (Nicky Case)
* [**Brilliant**](https://brilliant.org/courses/nutshell/) — Interactive math and science courses that teach by asking, not telling. (Free introductions available)
* [**DragonBox**](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/DragonBox) — A game that teaches algebra without ever showing an equation (until you've already mastered the concepts)
* [**This War of Mine**](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/This_War_of_Mine) — A game that communicates the civilian experience of war more powerfully than most textbooks
* [**New York Times Graphics**](https://www.nytimes.com/spotlight/graphics) — Data journalism that lets readers explore and discover patterns themselves

Notice how these all sit higher on the ICAP scale than a textbook. You're being told a story by *doing* something, not just *reading*.
]]></Markdown>
</Vertical>

<MarkupProblem id="inspiration_reflect" title="What Makes Them Work?">
Think about the examples above (or others you've seen). What is the primary reason interactive content is more effective for learning than a traditional essay or article?

( ) It's more entertaining {{ It often is, but some forms aren't. It's an answer, but it's not the best answer. }}
( ) It uses technology {{ Believe it or not, interactive content predates technology! Technology just makes it easier. }}
(x) It requires the audience to think, not just read
( ) It has better graphics {{ Interactive content can have no graphics, and a beautiful video is passive }}

[explanation]
Entertainment and graphics can help, but they're not the core. **The key is cognitive engagement.** When you have to answer a question, move a slider, make a choice, or write something — your brain is processing the material at a deeper level than when you're passively reading.

That's the ICAP principle at work. And it's what you're about to learn to create.
[/explanation]
  </MarkupProblem>

  <Vertical id="inspiration_share" title="Your Favorites">
    <Markdown><![CDATA[
Do you know of other examples? Things that communicated a point or told a story interactively? What made them effective?
    ]]></Markdown>
    <TextArea id="others" rows="4" placeholder="Share any interactive experiences you've found effective..." />
    <Markdown><![CDATA[
This isn't required — we'd genuinely love to know (so we can make sure out platform can do it too :) )!
    ]]></Markdown>
  </Vertical>

  <Vertical id="choose_topic" title="Your Topic">
    <Markdown><![CDATA[
## What Do You Want to Communicate?

Pick something you'd like to teach someone or convince someone of. Or something you'd like someone to learn. It could be:

* A concept from another class
* An opinion you feel strongly about
* Something you recently learned and found surprising
* A skill you know how to do

It doesn't matter much — but we'll keep referring back to this, so be genuine.
    ]]></Markdown>
    <TextArea id="comm" rows="3" placeholder="I want to communicate..." />
  </Vertical>

