  <Vertical id="design_intro" title="Design Your Sequence">
    <Markdown><![CDATA[
## Part 2: Design Your Sequence

You want to communicate:
> {{ @comm.value }}

Now you'll design an interactive sequence to teach this. An interactive sequence is just a series of steps — text, questions, reflections — that guide someone through your idea.

Think about the examples you saw. Instead of writing an essay *about* your topic, you'll create an *experience* that helps someone understand it. Ask leading questions. Let the reader discover the answer.

**Describe your sequence step by step in plain English.** What does the reader see first? What questions do you ask them? What do you want them to realize at each step?
    ]]></Markdown>
    <TextArea id="sequence_design" rows="10" placeholder="Step 1: Start with a surprising fact about...
Step 2: Ask the reader what they think causes...
Step 3: Show them the evidence...
Step 4: Ask them to apply the concept to...
Step 5: Have them reflect on..." />
  </Vertical>

  <Vertical id="llm_convert" title="Convert to OLX">
    <Markdown><![CDATA[
### See It Come to Life

When you're ready, click the button below. An AI will convert your plain-text design into OLX — the markup language this platform uses. You'll see the code *and* a live preview.

Don't worry about the code yet — we'll learn OLX in the next section. For now, just see what your design looks like as interactive content. Your plan:
> {{ @sequence_design.value }}

Convert this to **concrete and specific text and multiple choice questions**. There are richer forms of interaction, but this is an easy place to start. Please be specific!
    ]]></Markdown>
    <TextArea id="sequence_details" rows="10" />
    <Markdown>{{ wordcount(@sequence_details.value) }} / 20 words</Markdown>
    <ActionButton id="convert_btn" label="Convert My Design to OLX" dependsOn="wordcount(@sequence_details.value) >= 20">
      <LLMAction id="convert_action" target="student_olx"><![CDATA[You are converting a student's plain-text interactive sequence design into OLX markup. The student wants to communicate the following topic:

TOPIC: <Ref target="comm" />

THEIR DESIGN:
<Ref target="sequence_design" />

THEIR PLAN:
<Ref target="sequence_details" />


Convert this into valid OLX using ONLY these blocks:
- <Sequential> for multi-step sequences (with unique id and title attributes)
- <Markdown> for text content
- <MarkupProblem id="..." title="..."> for multiple choice questions. Use ( ) for wrong answers and (x) for correct. Include [explanation]...[/explanation] blocks.
- <TextArea id="..." rows="N" placeholder="..."/> for open-ended responses
- <Vertical> to group related content on one page

RULES:
- Every block that needs an id must have a UNIQUE id attribute (use descriptive names like "q_cause", "reflect_1", etc.)
- Keep the student's voice and intent. Don't add content they didn't describe.
- Make questions have obvious/leading answers when the student intended that.
- If the student's design is vague, make reasonable choices but keep it simple.
- Wrap everything in a single <Sequential> with id="student_sequence" and a descriptive title.
- Output ONLY valid OLX. No explanation, no markdown fences, no commentary.]]></LLMAction>
    </ActionButton>

    <CodeInput id="student_olx" language="olx" height="250px"><![CDATA[<Sequential id="student_sequence" title="My Interactive Sequence">
  <Markdown>
  Your converted OLX will appear here after you click "Convert My Design to OLX" above.

  Or you can start editing directly!
  </Markdown>
</Sequential>]]></CodeInput>

    <OlxSlot target="student_olx" debounce="200" id="olx_preview" chrome="true" title="Your Sequence">
      <Markdown>*Your live preview will appear here.*</Markdown>
    </OlxSlot>
  </Vertical>

  <Vertical id="design_feedback" title="Get Design Feedback">
    <Markdown>
### How Interactive Is Your Design?

Remember the ICAP framework? Let's see where your sequence falls. Click the button for feedback on how to make it more interactive.
    </Markdown>

    <ActionButton id="feedback_btn" label="Get Feedback on My Design" dependsOn="len(@student_olx.value) >= 30">
      <LLMAction id="feedback_action" target="design_feedback_display"><![CDATA[You are a learning design coach helping a student improve their interactive sequence. Evaluate their OLX content using the ICAP framework (Chi & Wylie, 2014):

- **Passive**: Reader just receives information (reading text)
- **Active**: Reader does something with the material (answering MCQs, highlighting)
- **Constructive**: Reader produces new output (writing explanations, drawing connections)
- **Interactive**: Reader builds on others' contributions or engages in dialogue

The student's topic: <Ref target="comm" />
Their original design: <Ref target="sequence_design" />
Their OLX: <Ref target="student_olx" />

Provide feedback in this format:

**ICAP Analysis**
For each step/block in their sequence, identify which ICAP level it represents (P/A/C/I). Use a simple list.

**What's Working**
1-2 specific things they did well (be encouraging and concrete).

**Level Up** (pick ONE concrete suggestion)
Suggest ONE specific change that would move an element up the ICAP scale. For example:
- A Passive text block → add a question to make it Active
- An Active MCQ → add a "explain your reasoning" TextArea to make it Constructive
- A solo activity → suggest how a peer could engage with it to make it Interactive

Keep it concise (under 200 words total). Use a warm, coaching tone. Do NOT rewrite their OLX — just describe the change.]]></LLMAction>
    </ActionButton>

    <LLMFeedback id="design_feedback_display" />
  </Vertical>

  <!-- ============================================================
       PART 3: LEARN OLX
       ============================================================ -->

<Markdown  id="olx_intro" title="Learning OLX"><![CDATA[
## Part 3: Learn OLX

You've already seen OLX in action — the AI generated some for you. Now let's understand how it works so you can edit and create it yourself.

OLX (Open Learning XML) is a markup language for interactive content. If you've ever seen HTML, it will look familiar. If not, don't worry — it's simpler than HTML.

The basic idea: everything is a **block** wrapped in **tags**, like `Markdown` below. Markdown is a syntax commonly used on wikis. You can read about it [on Daring Fireball](https://daringfireball.net/projects/markdown/basics). It's very easy-to-use.

```olx:playground
<Markdown>
Markdown is a **text** block, with some simple _formatting_.
</Markdown>
```

Blocks can have **attributes** — settings that change how they behave. For example, try changing the placeholder text or number of rows on this text area element:

```olx:playground
<TextArea id="response" rows="4" placeholder="Type here..." />
```

And blocks can be **nested** inside other blocks. The most common ones are `Vertical` (which has elements above each other) and `Sequential` (which is, like this page, a series students walk through with next / previous buttons). Try changing the `Vertical` to a `Sequential` and see what happens:

```olx:playground
<Vertical>
  <Markdown>A question:</Markdown>
  <TextArea id="answer" rows="2" />
</Vertical>
```

And one major annoyance. We often want to use characters like &amp; or &lt; in our Markdown. Unfortunately, a computer can't tell if you're starting to write `5 M 7` or the start of `</Markdown>`. You have three choices:
1. You can write `&amp;` for `&`, `&lt;` for `<`, and `&gt;` for `>`;
2. You can enclose your text in `<![CDATA[` followed by `​]]]]><![CDATA[>`; or
3. You can religiously avoid using those three characters in your text.

Starting out, we recommend #3, but this way, you'll at least know what `CDATA` and escape sequences are.

]]></Markdown>

  <Vertical id="olx_questions" title="Adding Questions">
    <Markdown><![CDATA[
### Adding Questions

A `<MarkupProblem>` lets you create multiple-choice questions with a compact syntax:

```olx:playground
<MarkupProblem id="my_question" title="My Question">
What color is the sky on a clear day?

( ) Green
(x) Blue
( ) Red

[explanation]
The sky appears blue due to Rayleigh scattering.
[/explanation]
</MarkupProblem>
```

- `( )` marks a wrong answer
- `(x)` marks the correct answer
- `[explanation]...[/explanation]` shows feedback after answering

On the ICAP scale, a question moves the reader from **Passive** (just reading) to **Active** (engaging with the material). Try editing the question below.
]]></Markdown>

    <CodeInput id="olx_question_practice" language="olx" height="200px"><![CDATA[<MarkupProblem id="practice_q" title="Practice Question">
Which ICAP level involves producing new output (not just selecting an answer)?

( ) Passive
( ) Active
(x) Constructive
( ) Interactive

[explanation]
Constructive learning means generating something new — an explanation, a diagram, a piece of writing. It goes beyond Active (selecting/manipulating) because the learner creates output that wasn't in the original material.
[/explanation]
</MarkupProblem>]]></CodeInput>

    <OlxSlot target="olx_question_practice" debounce="150" id="olx_question_preview">
      <Markdown>*Preview will appear here.*</Markdown>
    </OlxSlot>
  </Vertical>

  <Vertical id="olx_sequences" title="Sequences and Structure">
    <Markdown><![CDATA[
### Sequences and Structure

To create a multi-step experience (like this tutorial), wrap blocks in a `<Sequential>`:

```
<Sequential id="my_seq" title="My Sequence">
  <Markdown>Welcome! Click Next.</Markdown>

  <MarkupProblem id="q1" title="Question 1">
  What are we learning?
  ( ) Nothing
  (x) OLX authoring
  </MarkupProblem>

  <Markdown>Great job! You've finished.</Markdown>
</Sequential>
```

Each direct child of `<Sequential>` becomes a separate page with **Next** / **Back** buttons.

Use `<Vertical>` to group multiple blocks on the *same* page:

```
<Sequential id="my_seq" title="My Sequence">
  <Vertical>
    <Markdown>Here's a question and a text box on the same page:</Markdown>
    <TextArea id="response" rows="3" />
  </Vertical>
</Sequential>
```

Try building a short sequence in the editor below!
]]></Markdown>

    <CodeInput id="olx_seq_practice" language="olx" height="250px"><![CDATA[<Sequential id="mini_seq" title="My Mini Sequence">
  <Markdown>
  ## Welcome
  This is page 1. Click Next to continue.
  </Markdown>

  <MarkupProblem id="seq_q1" title="A Question">
  Is this page 2?

  (x) Yes!
  ( ) No

  [explanation]
  Each child of Sequential is a separate page.
  [/explanation]
  </MarkupProblem>

  <Vertical>
    <Markdown>## Page 3: Your Turn</Markdown>
    <TextArea id="seq_response" rows="3" placeholder="Write something here..." />
  </Vertical>
</Sequential>]]></CodeInput>

    <OlxSlot target="olx_seq_practice" debounce="200" id="olx_seq_preview">
      <Markdown>*Preview will appear here.*</Markdown>
    </OlxSlot>
  </Vertical>

  <Vertical id="olx_text_input" title="Text Input and References">
    <Markdown><![CDATA[
### Text Input and References

A `<TextArea>` lets readers write open-ended responses — moving from **Active** to **Constructive** on the ICAP scale:

```
<TextArea id="my_input" rows="4" placeholder="Write here..." />
```

The powerful part: you can **reference** what someone wrote using `{{ @id.value }}` in a Markdown block:

```
<Markdown>
You wrote: {{ @my_input.value }}
</Markdown>
```

This lets you create sequences where the reader's own words appear later — reflections, callbacks, building on their thinking. Try it in the editor below. Type something in the TextArea, then see it reflected.
]]></Markdown>

    <CodeInput id="olx_ref_practice" language="olx" height="220px"><![CDATA[<Sequential id="ref_demo" title="Reference Demo">
  <Vertical>
    <Markdown>## What's your favorite food?</Markdown>
    <TextArea id="fav_food" rows="2" placeholder="I love..." />
  </Vertical>

  <Markdown>
  You said your favorite food is:

  > {{ @fav_food.value }}

  Great choice!
  </Markdown>
</Sequential>]]></CodeInput>

    <OlxSlot target="olx_ref_practice" debounce="200" id="olx_ref_preview">
      <Markdown>*Preview will appear here.*</Markdown>
    </OlxSlot>
  </Vertical>

